>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/training/">Workouts & Training</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Recovery</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Treadmill After Knee Replacement</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        A treadmill can be a useful, predictable place to rebuild your walking after a knee replacement. The question is when, and the honest answer is: once your surgeon or physical therapist says so, usually after you are walking steadily with a cane or without an aid.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 28, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on published exercise guidance. General information, not medical advice.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#short-answer" class="btn-ghost">When You Can Start</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          After a total knee replacement, walking is the main event of your recovery. The American
          Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons (AAOS) puts it plainly in its patient exercise guide:
          proper walking is the best way to help your knee recover. A treadmill is simply one place
          to do that walking, with a flat surface, rails within reach and no kerbs. This guide sets
          out where it usually fits, what reputable sources say, and how to use one without setting
          your knee back. It is general information, not a rehab plan: your surgeon's and
          physical therapist's instructions come first every time.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#timeline">A Typical Timeline, Week by Week</a></li>
            <li><a href="#early-weeks">The Early Weeks: Why the Treadmill Usually Waits</a></li>
            <li><a href="#bike-first">The Bike Usually Comes First</a></li>
            <li><a href="#first-session">Your First Treadmill Sessions</a></li>
            <li><a href="#handrails">Holding the Handrails</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline">Incline: Leave It Until Later</a></li>
            <li><a href="#pain-swelling">Pain and Swelling: How to Tell If You Did Too Much</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warning-signs">Warning Signs: When to Stop and Call Your Surgeon</a></li>
            <li><a href="#progression">Building Up From Month Two Onwards</a></li>
            <li><a href="#long-term">Long Term: Walking Yes, Running Usually Not</a></li>
            <li><a href="#choosing-a-machine">What to Look For in a Treadmill for Knee Recovery</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verdict">The Verdict</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Most people are not ready for a treadmill in the first couple of weeks after a
            knee replacement, and many rehab programmes introduce one once you are walking steadily
            with a cane or without any aid.</strong> For a lot of people that falls somewhere in the
            second month, but the range is wide and your own surgeon's plan sets the date.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>First weeks:</strong> walking with a walker or crutches, short and often,
            plus the exercises your physical therapist gives you. The AAOS says many people move to
            a single cane or crutch at about 2 to 3 weeks, once they can walk and stand for more
            than 10 minutes without leaning on the walker.</li>
            <li><strong>Before the treadmill:</strong> a stationary or recumbent bike is the usual
            earlier machine, because it works the knee's bend without asking you to balance on a
            moving surface.</li>
            <li><strong>First treadmill sessions:</strong> the belt's slowest comfortable speed, a
            flat deck, five to ten minutes, handrails within reach and someone nearby.</li>
            <li><strong>Long term:</strong> walking is encouraged for life. Running and jogging are
            a different matter; the AAOS lists jogging among the high-impact activities to avoid,
            and many surgeons discourage running on a replaced knee.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If your knee is getting more swollen and painful from day to day, or you notice calf
            pain, fever or changes at the wound, stop and call your surgeon's office. The warning
            signs are listed in full further down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A Typical Timeline, Week by Week</h2>
<p>
            The table below pulls together the broad pattern described by the AAOS's OrthoInfo
            pages and by published hospital rehab guides. The bands overlap on purpose. People
            recover at very different rates depending on age, fitness before surgery, other health
            conditions and how the operation went, and a programme that moves faster or slower than
            this is not wrong. Treat it as a map of the usual order of things, not a schedule to
            hold yourself to.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[620px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical period</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Walking</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Treadmill</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Other machines</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Hospital to about week 2</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Walker or crutches; short, frequent walks on level ground; prescribed exercises daily</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Not usually</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">None at home unless your therapist says so</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">About weeks 2 to 6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Many move to a cane at around 2 to 3 weeks (AAOS); walks get longer and smoother</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Sometimes used in the clinic under supervision; at home only with your therapist's agreement</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Stationary or recumbent bike, often starting with backward pedalling or partial turns until the knee bends enough</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">About weeks 6 to 12</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Many people walk without an aid; distance and pace build</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Commonly introduced or progressed: slow, flat, short sessions growing to 15 to 30 minutes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Bike resistance increases gradually; the AAOS mentions adding tension at about 4 to 6 weeks</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">About 3 months onward</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Longer everyday walks; strength and balance work continue</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Longer sessions, brisker pace, gentle incline if your knee tolerates it</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Bike, elliptical and swimming as your surgeon allows</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Long term</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Walking as much as you like (AAOS)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Walking at any comfortable pace and grade; running generally discouraged</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700 align-top">Low-impact options remain the core</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two points about reading the table. First, the AAOS's activities page says to keep
            doing the exercises your physical therapist prescribed for at least two months after
            surgery, and that walking is not a substitute for them. A treadmill session is extra
            walking, not a replacement for your knee-bending and strengthening work. Second, swelling
            commonly lingers for weeks or months after a knee replacement, so a knee that still puffs
            up a little at the end of the day in week eight is not necessarily a sign that something
            is wrong. What matters is the trend, which we cover below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="early-weeks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Early Weeks: Why the Treadmill Usually Waits</h2>
<p>
            In the first weeks you are walking with a walker or crutches, and the AAOS's exercise
            guide describes the goal as walking rhythmically and smoothly without hurrying. Your
            therapist will be working on how much your knee straightens and bends, on your thigh
            muscles, and on a heel-to-toe walking pattern rather than a stiff-legged shuffle.
          </p>
          <p>
            A treadmill does not suit that stage well at home, for three practical reasons.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A walker does not work on a moving belt.</strong> A walker needs a still
            floor. On a treadmill you would be holding the side rails instead, which is a different
            kind of support.</li>
            <li><strong>The belt sets the pace.</strong> On the floor you can stop, pause or shorten
            a step whenever the knee complains. On a belt, even a slow one, you have to keep up or
            press stop.</li>
            <li><strong>Getting on and off is a step up.</strong> Most home treadmills have a deck
            several inches off the floor, and stepping up and down onto it with a freshly operated
            knee is a balance and strength task in its own right.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of that means a treadmill is dangerous for someone recovering from knee surgery.
            Many outpatient physical therapy clinics do use one, with a therapist standing next to
            you, a safety key clipped on and the speed under their control. The difference at home is
            that you are usually on your own. So the sensible order is floor walking first, then the
            treadmill once you are steady.
          </p>
          <p>
            A good working test, borrowed from the AAOS's own cane guidance: if you can walk and stand
            for more than ten minutes without leaning on an aid, you are in a much better position to
            try a belt. Ask your therapist to watch your first session if you can, or at least to
            give you a starting speed and time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bike-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Bike Usually Comes First</h2>
<p>
            Ask most physical therapists which machine to use early in knee replacement recovery and
            the answer is a bike. The AAOS's exercise guide calls riding an <a href="/best-exercise-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>exercise bike</a> an excellent
            activity to help you regain muscle strength and knee mobility, and its activities page
            mentions that your doctor may have you riding a stationary bicycle at physical therapy.
          </p>
          <p>
            The reason is mechanical. Pedalling takes the knee through a large, repeated bend while
            the seat carries your body weight, so it works on range of motion without the balance
            demands of walking. If a full revolution is not yet possible, therapists commonly have
            people rock back and forth, or pedal backwards, until the knee loosens enough to go all the
            way round. The AAOS guide also suggests raising the tension gradually as you get stronger,
            at about 4 to 6 weeks.
          </p>
          <p>
            For home use, a <strong>recumbent bike</strong> is often the easier choice: the
            step-through frame and backrest make getting on and off simpler, and you are less likely to
            wobble. Our guide to the <a href="/best-recumbent-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best recumbent bikes</a> covers low step-through heights and seat adjustment, which matter more than resistance
            levels when you are recovering. Set the seat so your knee is almost straight at the bottom
            of the stroke, as the AAOS describes, and move it closer only as your bend improves.
          </p>
          <p>
            Bike and treadmill are not either-or. Once you are cleared for both, many people use the
            bike to loosen the knee and the treadmill to practise walking. If you have only one
            machine and the choice is still open, the bike is the more forgiving early tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-session" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Your First Treadmill Sessions</h2>
<p>
            Once your surgeon or therapist has said treadmill walking is fine, keep the first week or
            two deliberately boring. The point is to practise a good walking pattern on a moving
            surface, not to get a workout.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Speed.</strong> Start at the lowest speed that lets you walk naturally. On many
            home treadmills the belt starts at 0.5 mph, and for most people recovering from knee
            surgery something around 1 to 1.5 mph is a comfortable first setting. Too slow can feel
            awkward, because you end up stepping in an unnatural, halting way; too fast makes you
            shorten your stride and limp. Ask your therapist for a number if they have watched you
            walk. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-speed-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill speed for seniors</a> explains how to find a comfortable setting by feel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Duration.</strong> Five to ten minutes is a sensible first session. The AAOS's
            general guidance suggests your surgeon and therapist may ask you to exercise for 20 to 30
            minutes daily, or even two to three times a day, and to walk regularly; the treadmill
            minutes should fit inside that plan, not be piled on top of it. If ten minutes leaves your
            knee comfortable that evening and the next morning, add a couple of minutes next time.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline.</strong> Zero. Keep the deck flat until your therapist says otherwise.
            The reasons are covered in their own section below.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Safety key.</strong> Clip it to your clothing every time, so the belt stops if you
            drift back or lose your footing. Make sure you know where the stop button is before the
            belt moves.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Getting on.</strong> Stand on the side rails, not the belt, while it starts at its
            lowest speed, then step on with your stronger leg first. Reverse the order to get off:
            slow the belt right down or stop it, step onto the side rails, then down to the floor. If
            your machine's manual says to start on a stationary belt, follow the manual. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-on-and-off-a-treadmill-safely/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to get on and off a treadmill safely</a> goes through each step in detail.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Shoes and company.</strong> Wear supportive, closed-toe shoes rather than socks or
            slippers. For the first few sessions, have someone in the house who knows you are on the
            machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What to focus on.</strong> Heel down first, then roll through to the toes, with
            your knee straightening as your heel lands. Aim for equal step lengths on both legs. Many
            people favour the new knee by taking a short, quick step on it; a slow belt and a mirror,
            or a family member watching from the side, helps you notice that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="handrails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Holding the Handrails</h2>
<p>
            General treadmill advice often tells people to walk without holding on, so the legs do
            all the work. That advice is aimed at healthy exercisers gripping the rails to cope with
            a speed or incline that is too much for them. It is not a rule for someone a few weeks
            out from knee surgery.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>In early recovery, use the rails for balance.</strong> A light hold gives you a
            point of contact while your balance and confidence come back, and it lets you react if the
            knee gives a twinge. What you want to avoid is leaning hard on the rails to take weight off
            the leg. If you need to grip that hard, the speed is too high or you are not yet ready for
            the treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            Over the weeks, most people progress from two hands to one hand to fingertips, and then
            to arms swinging freely with a rail close by. Your therapist may set specific goals here.
            Our explainer on <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether you should hold the handrails on a treadmill</a> covers the difference between balance support and leaning in more depth.
          </p>
          <p>
            If your treadmill has only short stubs of handrail by the console, it is a weaker choice
            for this stage. Machines with long side rails running back along the deck make it much
            easier to keep a hand on something without twisting or reaching forward. Our roundup of <a href="/best-treadmills-with-handrails/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with full-length handrails</a> lists models built that way, and our guide to <a href="/ksports-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Ksports treadmills</a> covers a budget rehab
            walking treadmill with multi-grip rails along the top, middle and sides.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Incline: Leave It Until Later</h2>
<p>
            Incline is usually the best way for a healthy walker to make a session harder. After a
            knee replacement it is something to add later and in small amounts, for a few reasons.
          </p>
          <p>
            Walking uphill asks the thigh muscles to work harder and keeps the knee more bent through
            each step. In the weeks after surgery the thigh muscles are typically weak and the knee
            often does not yet straighten fully, so a slope can push you into a bent-knee, short-stride
            pattern that therapists are trying to train out. It also tends to raise the effort, and the
            soreness afterwards, faster than you might expect.
          </p>
          <p>
            A reasonable approach, once your therapist agrees, is to add incline only after you can
            walk 20 to 30 minutes on the flat with no increase in swelling the next day. Start at 1 to
            2 percent, keep the speed the same or lower it slightly, and hold it for a few minutes
            within a flat session rather than the whole walk. If your knee aches more the next day,
            go back to flat for a while.
          </p>
          <p>
            Decline settings, which some treadmills have, are a different case. Walking downhill puts
            more load through the front of the knee and is generally harder on it. Leave decline alone
            unless your physical therapist has specifically included it in your plan. For the bigger
            picture on how walking and running load the joint, see our guide on <a href="/are-treadmills-bad-for-your-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether treadmills are bad for your knees</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pain-swelling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Pain and Swelling: How to Tell If You Did Too Much</h2>
<p>
            Some discomfort is a normal part of recovering from a knee replacement, and you will not
            get through rehab without working the knee. The question is whether the treadmill is
            helping or tipping you backwards. Physical therapists commonly use a simple rule of thumb
            that is worth asking your own therapist about.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>During the walk:</strong> mild aching that does not build is generally
            acceptable. Sharp pain, pain that grows minute by minute, or a limp that gets worse are
            signs to slow down or stop.</li>
            <li><strong>That evening:</strong> some extra soreness and swelling that settles with
            rest, elevation and ice is common.</li>
            <li><strong>The next morning:</strong> this is the key check. If your knee is back to
            where it was before the session, the amount was about right. If it is noticeably more
            swollen, stiffer or more painful than the day before, you did too much. Cut the next
            session back by about a third or take a day off the treadmill.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The AAOS's exercise guide describes elevating the leg and using an ice pack or ice wrapped
            in a towel to relieve swelling, applied for around 20 minutes and then removed for 20
            minutes. Follow whatever icing routine your own team gave you, and never put ice directly on
            the skin or on an unhealed wound.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep a short log: date, minutes, speed, and how the knee felt the next morning. It takes
            seconds, it stops you guessing, and it gives your therapist something concrete to look at
            at your next appointment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warning-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Warning Signs: When to Stop and Call Your Surgeon</h2>
<p>
            The AAOS's guidance on activities after knee replacement lists signs that you should tell
            your doctor about right away. They are not specific to treadmill use, but walking more is
            exactly the point at which people tend to notice them, so they are worth knowing before you
            start.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Possible blood clot in the leg:</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Pain in your leg or calf that is not related to your incision</li>
            <li>Tenderness or redness above or below your knee</li>
            <li>Increasing swelling of your calf, ankle or foot</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Possible infection:</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Persistent fever (the AAOS gives higher than 100°F)</li>
            <li>Shaking chills</li>
            <li>Increasing redness or swelling around the wound, or drainage from it</li>
            <li>Increasing knee pain with both activity and rest</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Call 911 or your local emergency number</strong> if you have sudden shortness of
            breath, sudden chest pain, or chest pain when you cough. The AAOS lists these as signs that a
            clot may have travelled to the lungs, which is rare but life-threatening.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also stop and contact your surgeon's office if you have a fall, feel or hear something
            give way in the knee, or have a sudden jump in pain that does not settle with rest. Do not
            try to walk through any of these to see whether they pass.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="progression" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Building Up From Month Two Onwards</h2>
<p>
            Once short, flat sessions feel routine, progress one thing at a time and give each change
            about a week before the next. A sensible order is time first, then speed, then incline.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Time:</strong> add two to five minutes a session until you reach 20 to 30
            minutes. Two shorter walks in a day are fine, and often better than one long one in the
            early months.</li>
            <li><strong>Speed:</strong> raise it 0.1 or 0.2 mph at a time, checking that your stride
            stays even. A pace that makes you limp or shorten your step on the operated side is too
            fast for now, whatever the number says.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline:</strong> introduce small grades only after the first two are
            comfortable, as described above.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Hospital for Special Surgery's patient guidance on returning to exercise after joint
            replacement makes the same general point: build up slowly rather than jumping from short
            walks to long ones. For a structured way to do that, our <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workout for seniors</a> and our guide to <a href="/how-long-should-seniors-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long seniors should walk on a treadmill</a> both use gentle, time-based progressions that suit people coming back from surgery,
            whatever their age.
          </p>
          <p>
            Keep doing your strengthening and stretching exercises alongside the walking. Stronger
            thigh and hip muscles make walking easier on the new knee, and they are what lets you
            walk farther without limping.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="long-term" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Long Term: Walking Yes, Running Usually Not</h2>
<p>
            For the long term, the AAOS's message is encouraging about walking: its guidance on
            activities after knee replacement says to walk as much as you would like. It also
            recommends lower-impact fitness activities such as golfing, bicycling and pickleball, and
            describes swimming as an excellent low-impact option once the wound has healed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Running is a different story. The AAOS lists high-impact activities such as jumping,
            jogging and skiing among those to avoid after a knee replacement, and many surgeons advise
            patients not to run on a replaced knee. The general concern is that repeated high loads may
            wear the implant faster. Some surgeons are more permissive with experienced runners, so if
            running matters to you, raise it with your own surgeon rather than deciding on your own.
            On a treadmill, this simply means the walking range is your territory.
          </p>
          <p>
            That still leaves plenty of room for a proper workout: a brisk walk on a gentle incline
            raises your heart rate well without any impact. If you are wondering whether a treadmill is
            the right long-term machine at all, our guide on <a href="/is-a-treadmill-good-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether a treadmill is good for seniors</a> weighs it against bikes and ellipticals, and our page on <a href="/treadmill-walking-with-arthritis/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill walking with arthritis</a> is useful if your other knee or your hips are arthritic too.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing-a-machine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Look For in a Treadmill for Knee Recovery</h2>
<p>
            If you are buying a treadmill with recovery in mind, the features that matter are the
            unglamorous ones.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A low starting speed</strong> of 0.5 mph, adjustable in 0.1 mph steps, so you
            can set exactly the pace your therapist suggests.</li>
            <li><strong>A low step-up height</strong>, so getting on and off asks less of the knee.</li>
            <li><strong>Long handrails</strong> along both sides of the deck, not just short grips by the
            console.</li>
            <li><strong>A cushioned, stable deck</strong> that does not bounce or shift underfoot.</li>
            <li><strong>Large, easy stop controls</strong> and a clip-on safety key.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for bad knees</a> roundup focuses on cushioning and deck design, and <a href="/best-walking-machines-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our picks of walking machines for seniors</a> prioritise low speeds and handrails. A walking pad can work later in recovery, but most have
            no rails at all, so check before buying; our guide to <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads for seniors</a> covers the few that add a support bar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Verdict</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A treadmill fits into knee replacement recovery once you are walking steadily on
            the floor, usually with a cane or no aid, and once your surgeon or therapist has agreed.</strong> For many people that is somewhere in the second month, with a bike as the usual earlier
            machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            Start flat, slow and short, hold the rails for balance, and let the next-morning check on
            your knee decide how quickly you progress. Add time before speed and speed before incline.
            Learn the warning signs before you start, and call your surgeon's office rather than
            walking through them. In the long run, walking is something the AAOS encourages as much as
            you like; running is something to discuss with your surgeon first.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes on the Treadmill After Knee Replacement</h2>
          <p>Most setbacks come from doing too much too soon, or from copying general treadmill advice that was never written for someone recovering from surgery.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Starting before you are steady on the floor</h3>
              <p>A moving belt is harder than a still floor. If you still need a walker, or cannot stand and walk for ten minutes without leaning on an aid, keep walking at home and on the bike until your therapist says you are ready for the treadmill.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Judging the session by how it felt at the time</h3>
              <p>Knees often feel fine during a walk and complain later. Check the next morning instead: if the knee is more swollen or stiffer than the day before, the session was too much. Cut the next one back by about a third rather than pushing on.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Adding incline to make it harder</h3>
              <p>Incline is a good tool for healthy walkers but tends to push a recovering knee into a bent, short-stride pattern. Add time first and speed second, and try small grades only once flat walking is comfortable and your therapist agrees.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Letting treadmill time replace your exercises</h3>
              <p>The AAOS says walking is not a substitute for the exercises your physical therapist prescribed. The knee-bending and strengthening work is what lets you walk well, so keep it going alongside the treadmill for as long as your plan says.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The takeaway</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Use a treadmill after knee replacement once you are walking
            steadily on the floor and your surgeon or therapist agrees</strong>, which for many people
            is somewhere in the second month. A bike usually comes first.
          </p>
          <p>
            Start flat, slow and short, hold the rails for balance, and let the next-morning check decide
            your progress. Learn the warning signs before you begin, and see our guide to <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmills for bad knees</a> if you are choosing a machine.
          </p>
        </div>

