        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>When can I walk on a treadmill after knee replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually once you are walking steadily on level ground with a cane or without any aid, and your surgeon or physical therapist has agreed. For many people that falls in the second month after surgery, though some clinics use a treadmill earlier under supervision and some people need longer. Your own surgeon's plan takes precedence over any general timeline.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed should I walk on a treadmill after knee replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Start at the slowest speed that still lets you walk naturally, often around 1 to 1.5 mph on a flat deck for five to ten minutes. Increase by 0.1 or 0.2 mph at a time only when your stride is even on both legs and your knee is no more swollen the next morning. Ask your therapist for a specific starting speed if they have watched you walk.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill or a stationary bike better after knee replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              In the early weeks, most rehab programmes favour the bike. The AAOS calls riding an exercise bike an excellent way to regain knee strength and mobility, and it carries your body weight while you work on bending the knee. A treadmill usually comes later, once you are walking steadily. Many people use both once they are cleared.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I use incline on a treadmill after knee replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Leave the deck flat at first. Walking uphill keeps the knee more bent and asks more of the thigh muscles, which are usually weak after surgery. Once you can walk 20 to 30 minutes on the flat without extra swelling the next day, and your therapist agrees, try short blocks at 1 to 2 percent. Avoid decline settings unless your therapist includes them.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I run on a treadmill after knee replacement?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Many surgeons advise against it. The AAOS lists jogging among the high-impact activities to avoid after a knee replacement, and it encourages walking, cycling, swimming and other lower-impact activities instead. Some surgeons take a different view for experienced runners, so ask your own surgeon before trying.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What symptoms after treadmill walking mean I should call my surgeon?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The AAOS lists calf or leg pain not related to the incision, tenderness or redness above or below the knee, and increasing swelling of the calf, ankle or foot as possible clot signs, and persistent fever over 100°F, shaking chills, wound drainage or increasing redness as possible infection signs. Call emergency services for sudden shortness of breath or chest pain.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

